cing restrictions such as  limiting opening times for restaurants  perhaps and venues vaccinating children  is another measure authorities are  taking to reduce their severity of  infections  a quick pinch  and it's all done  these kids in denmark are getting  vaccinated against covid19  i got the jab because of the new corona  variant and i don't want to be so sick  that i can't eat dinner  omicron is spreading at a fast speed in  denmark  infection rates are hitting daily record  highs  pushing more and more parents to get  their children jabbed  to slow the spread of the virus the  danish government has announced that  cinemas theatres and concert halls will  be closed  restaurant opening hours will also be  restricted  in neighboring germany the health  minister has raised the alarm  i'm assuming a massive fifth wave  we have to prepare ourselves for a  challenge that we have not yet faced in  this form  infection rates have been rising rapidly  across germany too  the easy spread of omicron would send  rates soaring even higher  as a result the german government has  now classified denmark and other  countries including france and norway as  high-risk areas  travelers will have to quarantine for 10  days upon arrival  unless they have been vaccinated or  recovered from a coronavirus infection  kai cooper schmitz is a journalist and  molecular biologist he joins us from  right here in berlin hello kai so are  travel bans and restrictions really  justified  well i think we have to look at these  two things separately and  there's a lot of uncertainty still about  what exactly the impact of omicron will  be what we do know is that it spreads  really really fast and that it is going  to lead to a lot of infections so that's  the reason that for instance in a  country like germany where we still have  a high number of uh you know unimmunized  people we need to be really careful and  that's the reason that restrictions are  being put in place and that seems to be  th